# Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, E-books

Writing the recent post about Literate Programming, and the bit at the end about its creator, Donald E. Knuth, made me nostalgic to read The Art of Computer Programming, again. Originally conceived as a single book with 12 chapters, it is now an only-partially-complete set of 7 volumes, not quite 3½ of which have been completed/published, each one containing only two (enormous) chapters, each.

As mentioned, in order to create this work, he had to create the system with which it was typeset, the font format for that typesetting engine, and the fonts themselves. While creating the ponderous work itself, he also could not satisfy himself with existing programming languages, so he created a new one, specifically for writing the concrete code examples within the book in (a machine language, absent a machine—“MIX”). And then later rewrote/modernized it (“MMIX”).

As its author is a mathematician, the books often include very detailed and rigorous discussion of underlying mathematics and theory, and the included exercises include ones for advanced mathematicians, and even in some cases unresolved research problems. However, he intended (intends) this work to be of use to the general programmer who may not have such a mathematical background, and so he wrote each section to start with the simplest explanations, and become progressively advanced, so that the reader may recognize whenever things are getting a bit too heady, and skip over them. Similarly, where he provides exercises for the reader (as the books are also intended for the possibility of use in a classroom), he rates their difficulty (10 being straightforward and requiring a few minutes, 20 being a problem of average difficulty taking ~ a half hour, and 46 or higher being an unresolved research problem). Exercises requiring an understanding of advanced mathematics are explicitly marked with an “M”.

In practice, and despite his lengthy explanation of all of this in the book’s introductory chapters, I suspect the average programmer attempting to read the book still will tend to experience their eyes glazing over, and soon move on to other books. Even his prose tends to bely the fact that this book was very much written by a mathematician.

But for me, The Art of Computer Programming represents a heaping, concentrated dose of much of what I find myself increasingly drawn to, as the world of programming gets sucked into the vibe-coding vortex, and programming becomes increasingly more about supervising automatic code generation, and the art of examining and designing approaches to solving problems, with a human brain, appears increasingly to fall by the wayside.

It is a celebration of mental exercise, of precision in understanding, and of academia. It is also perfectly content to inject the odd bit of dry humor (such as including a flowchart, and accompanying algorithmic breakdown, for the enjoyment of the books, or the disclaimer when explaining the expected time to complete exercises of different ratings, of increased time requirements in the event that the TV is on). It is also simultaneously, both ponderously academic, and unquestionably _weird_ (as any book on programming that insists on inventing its own machine language to explain things in must be).

## Obtaining the e-books

I already possess physical copies of the work, Volumes 1-3, as well as a few fascicles (individually-published segments) from Volume 4. However, reading paper books is increasingly inconvenient to me, as I am very accustomed to the convenience of e-Ink e-readers—and the larger and heavier the book is, the more I am inclined to prefer it in digital form.

Of course, some kinds of books, mathematical ones in particular, or any others that rely on layout and formatting for more than just aesthetics, don’t do as well as reflowable ePubs or the like, and more or less demand to be viewed as non-reflowable PDFs. This work is certainly no exception. Letter- (or A4-) sized pages, meanwhile, don’t view easily on more-compact, typical eInk readers. Fortunately my ReMarkable 2 has a large enough display to make reading PDFs convenient (...enough).

While trawling through Knuth’s Stanford website, I found a link to the official source for buying copies of the books in hyperlinked PDF format (with a warning that unofficial/misformatted PDF copies have also been found at various places).
=> https://www-cs-faculty.stanford.edu/~knuth/ Donald E. Knuth’s Standford home page
=> http://www.informit.com/taocp Pearson InformIT bookstore’s TAOCP page, where may be found both physical and ebook copies of the works

I immediately lusted hard after these, and bought the full set (as currently available—Volumes 1-3, Volumes 4A & 4B, a fascicle on the updated MMIX language, and The MMIX Suplement (not actually by Knuth), providing reworked program examples from Volumes 1-3, originally written in MIX, rewritten in the newer MMIX language). I also wished to buy a copy of Concrete Mathematics, which he co-authored with Ronald L. Graham, and Oren Patashnik, which I have not previously read any part of, but shall have to wait until my finances recover.

Aside from those, I also found ebook copies of his 5-volume series, “Computers & Typesetting”, which includes tutorial-style manuals for TeX and Metafont (his typesetting and font-description languages), and the actual literate-program (see my preceding post on Literate Programming) source-code-as-literature program text for those programs (written in WEB, a literate-programming language combining TeX with Pascal). (The fifth volume of the series is on his computer font, Computer Modern, included with TeX (and consequently, used throughout these books, as well as TAOCP and other works).)

There is also Volume 4, Fascicle 7, which is not included in Volumes 4A and 4B (it forms what is expected to be the first third of “4C” so I should probably correct my oversight in not having included that in my purchase... There, done. Not on the bookstore, but available from his website, are “pre-fascicles”—“alpha” versions of upcoming/planned fascicles of Volume 4, provided primarily to invite feedback/corrections from experts in the field (they are provided as gzipped PostScript files, which can be decompressed and converted to (non-hyperlinked) PDF if necessary). Pre-fascicle 9b seems particularly compelling: “A Potpourri of Puzzles”! I may skip ahead to enjoy that for a bit, before continuing with my recent restart at Volume 1.

It's worth noting that the ebooks from the InformIT store come with both a PDF edition and an ePub edition, both together. As mentioned, the PDF is superior, and in fact the ePub versions are decidedly defective, missing text in spots (even in the prose!). Knuth’s site warns of the defective ePubs, declaring only the PDFs to have been reviewed and approved by himself, and laments them having been published in that form at all.

The missing text from the ePubs is not random, but tends to be from some tables, and ordered lists. However, it _is_ reflowable, unlike the PDF, and this makes it sometimes more convenient for reading large sections of prose, since I can adjust the font size and reflow it for a more relaxed reading experience. Still, this is only useful if I also follow along on the PDF (ePub on my Kobo eReader, PDF on the ReMarkable 2), so that I can make sense of ordered lists, tables, mathematical formulas, and program code. On the whole, it’s often easiest just to put on my reading glasses and stick with the PDF.

## Particular Advantages of the ReMarkable

The ReMarkable 2 is particularly well-suited for reading large books that include exercises. The reader, which is also designed for freehand writing and drawing, facilitates the insertion of new/additional “note” pages in viewed PDF documents, between those in the original, so that I can keep my handworked problem sheets together with the original exercise pages of the book. One can categorize book pages (both original and reader-added) via tags (_categorized_ bookmarks!), which makes it easy to quickly locate exercise sections as well as the answers in the back. And you can highlight passages, and scrawl notes in the margins, without defacing a physical book (nor even damaging the PDF document that backs the modifications!)


